question: Since no fixed-k cap on the top draws meets both halves, does a statistic acting on the whole ordered tail do better? Compare two variants of the cycle 75 gate at n=25, 50 under t(3): (a) a trimmed sum dropping the top m=ceil(0.1n) AD terms by |draw| rank, (b) a rank-weighted sum with weight r^-1 (and r^-2) on the term of |draw| rank r. For each report var/mean^2, the top-1% AD share of the top 3 draws, and normal-calibrated t(3) power at n=25, 50, 100 against the uncapped gate (k=0 baseline rows in the same table). Does either reach var/mean^2 at most 2/3 while keeping power within 0.02? If not, report the var/mean^2 vs power-loss frontier across the variants. Report seed and sims per n.
learning: No fixed-k cap meets both halves. Winsorizing the top k draws reaches var/mean^2 at most 2/3 (k=2 at n=25, k=3 at n=50) but removes the t(3) signal at the same rate: k=1 already costs 0.17-0.20 power. A joint share cap on the top k AD terms keeps power within 0.02 yet stalls at var/mean^2 1.0-1.3 for k>=2, barely moving from k=2 to k=5. Rank splits show whichever draw sets the cap inherits the dominance, so the excess is the whole ordered tail, not any count of top draws.
